My remark was partly meant as a joke, but I feel there is some truth to 
the statement 'nethack is a puzzle game'. It isn't, of course, but it 
has some puzzle elements. For example, at the beginning of the game, you 
don't know what any of the items do. You have to figure this out from 
various clues and experiments. Granted, this is a different type of 
puzzle than Sokoban, but it's a puzzle. Another thing I consider a 
puzzle is a dangerous situation. Say you just stepped on a magic trap 
and are surrounded by monsters and blind. Since nethack is turn based, 
you can take your time, look at all your items, figure out a solution to 
your situation. There may not be one, and you may need more information, 
but to me, there's a strong puzzle element here.

> However, we're probably just defining puzzle games differently.

As I said, I didn't mean to imply that nethack was a pure puzzle game. 
Nethack also isn't a first person shooter. Nethack is a roguelike. 
Deciding what a roguelike is, is pretty hard, if not impossible. I just 
noted that nethack has more puzzle elements.

> Regardless, what I was saying is what David Ploog has now said better in a 
